From: Hyunho Song <songhh06101@gmail.com>
Subject: ipc/msg: serialize receive permission checks with IPC_SET
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 12:28:21 +0900

do_msgrcv() checks read permission before taking the queue lock.  IPC_SET
can revoke access and pass through expunge_all() between that check and
insertion into q_receivers.  The new receiver can then sleep through the
revocation and accept a later message, even if senders skip receivers that
IPC_SET found on the list.

Check read permission under the same queue lock that protects IPC_SET and
waiter insertion, as do_msgsnd() already does for write permission.  On
denial, release the lock through the existing out_unlock0 path.

Signed-off-by: Hyunho Song <songhh06101@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Cc: Manfred Spraul <manfred@colorfullife.com>
Cc: Davidlohr Bueso <dave@stgolabs.net>
Tested-by: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Cc: <stable@vger.kernel.org>

 ipc/msg.c |    6 +++---
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/ipc/msg.c~ipc-msg-serialize-receive-permission-checks-with-ipc_set
+++ a/ipc/msg.c
@@ -1135,11 +1135,11 @@ static long do_msgrcv(int msqid, void __
 	for (;;) {
 		struct msg_receiver msr_d;
 
+		ipc_lock_object(&msq->q_perm);
+
 		msg = ERR_PTR(-EACCES);
 		if (ipcperms(ns, &msq->q_perm, S_IRUGO))
-			goto out_unlock1;
-
-		ipc_lock_object(&msq->q_perm);
+			goto out_unlock0;
 
 		/* raced with RMID? */
 		if (!ipc_valid_object(&msq->q_perm)) {
